<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>geometric sequence q3</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>The number of members in a school club in the first three years was \( {@e1@} \), \( {@e2@} \) and \( {@e3@} \). The numbers form a geometric progression (GP), and \(x\) is a whole number.</p>

<p>(a) Find the value of \(x\).</p>
<p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>

<p>(b) Find the common ratio, \(r\).</p>
<p>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>

<p>(c) Write an expression for the number of members in year \(n\). Use \(n\) in your answer.</p>
<p>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p>

<p>(d) In which year does the number of members first become more than {@cap@}?</p>
<p>[[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>The first three terms are \( {@e1@} \), \( {@e2@} \) and \( {@e3@} \). They form a geometric progression (GP).</p>

<p>(a) <strong>Value of \(x\):</strong></p>
<p>In a GP, the ratio between neighbouring terms is the same:</p>
<p>\[ \frac{T_2}{T_1} = \frac{T_3}{T_2} \]</p>
<p>Multiply both sides by \(T_1 \times T_2\). This gives \( (T_2)^2 = T_1 \times T_3 \):</p>
<p>\[ ({@e2@})^2 = ({@e1@})({@e3@}) \]</p>
<p>Expand both sides:</p>
<p>\[ {@lhs@} = {@rhs@} \]</p>
<p>The \(x^2\) terms are the same on both sides, so they cancel. Take the \(x\) terms to the right side and the numbers to the left side:</p>
<p>\[ {@cons@} = {@-coef@}x \]</p>
<p>Divide both sides by {@-coef@}:</p>
<p>\[ x = \frac{ {@cons@} }{ {@-coef@} } = {@ta1@} \]</p>

<p>(b) <strong>Common ratio:</strong></p>
<p>Put \( x = {@xx@} \) into the terms:</p>
<p>\[ T_1 = {@t1@}, \qquad T_2 = {@t2@}, \qquad T_3 = {@t3@} \]</p>
<p>Divide a term by the term before it:</p>
<p>\[ r = \frac{ {@t2@} }{ {@t1@} } = {@ta2@} \]</p>
<p>Check: \( \frac{ {@t3@} }{ {@t2@} } = {@ta2@} \). The ratio is the same.</p>

<p>(c) <strong>Members in year \(n\):</strong></p>
<p>Here \( a = {@a@} \) and \( r = {@r@} \). Put these into \( T_n = ar^{n-1} \):</p>
<p>\[ T_n = {@a@} \times {@r@}^{n-1} \]</p>

<p>(d) <strong>First year with more than {@cap@} members:</strong></p>
<p>First find the value of \(n\) that gives exactly {@cap@} members. Put \( T_n = {@cap@} \) into the rule:</p>
<p>\[ {@a@} \times {@r@}^{n-1} = {@cap@} \]</p>
<p>Divide both sides by {@a@}:</p>
<p>\[ {@r@}^{n-1} = {@cap/a@} \]</p>
<p>The unknown \(n\) is in the power, so take logarithms of both sides:</p>
<p>\[ (n-1)\log {@r@} = \log {@cap/a@} \]</p>
<p>\[ n - 1 = \frac{\log {@cap/a@}}{\log {@r@}} \]</p>
<p>\[ n = 1 + \frac{\log {@cap/a@}}{\log {@r@}} = {@dispdp(n_exact,2)@} \]</p>
<p>\(n\) must be a whole number of years. The number of members is exactly {@cap@} at \( n = {@dispdp(n_exact,2)@} \), so it is more than {@cap@} only for years after that.</p>
<p>Year {@n_low@} is too early, because it is less than {@dispdp(n_exact,2)@}. The smallest whole number bigger than {@dispdp(n_exact,2)@} is {@ta4@}.</p>
<p>So the number of members first becomes more than {@cap@} in year {@ta4@}.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
